<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shrinathji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shrinathji is a form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> depicted as a seven-year-old child lifting Govardhan Hill, worshipped as the principal deity of the Pushtimarg tradition founded by Vallabhacharya. The image, said to have been found on Govardhan Hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, was moved in 1672 to Mewar to escape Mughal hostility and installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a>, which became one of the major pilgrimage centres of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shrinathji.jpg" alt="Shrinathji" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathdwara Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrinathji_Vitthalanath_Birthday_Shringar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
